  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Los Angeles fired 1,400 shells at an empty sky. The 25th of February, 1942. Sirens at 2:25 in the morning. The guns opened up at 3:16.",
        "visual_query": "1940s los angeles skyline night",
        "visual_fallback": "night city skyline"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Searchlights swept the coast. Batteries fired for roughly an hour. Gunners reported aircraft overhead, and four of them shot down. Nothing ever fell.",
        "visual_query": "wartime searchlights night sky",
        "visual_fallback": "searchlight beams"
      },
      {
        "narration": "No bombs. No wreckage. Five people died \u2014 three in blackout car crashes, two of heart attacks. So who exactly were they shooting at?",
        "visual_query": "vintage american city street 1940s",
        "visual_fallback": "old city street"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Navy Secretary Frank Knox called it a false alarm the next day. War nerves. Nobody believed him, and the papers ran cover-up theories for decades.",
        "visual_query": "1940s newspaper printing press",
        "visual_fallback": "newspaper press"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 1983 the Air Force put it on the record. A stray weather balloon started it \u2014 then searchlights and shell bursts manufactured targets out of smoke.",
        "visual_query": "weather balloon launch sky",
        "visual_fallback": "weather balloon"
      }
    ],
